When you fetch a `.glb` file, you should handle it as binary data instead of trying to parse it as text or JSON. Here's how you can adjust your fetch in TypeScript (assuming you're working within an Angular component given the file name `make-avatar.component.ts`) to correctly handle the binary data of the `.glb` file:

### Modifying the Fetch Operation

You likely have something like this in your current code, which defaults to assuming the response is JSON:

```typescript
this.http.get(`https://api.readyplayer.me/v2/avatars/[avatar-id].glb?preview=true`)
  .subscribe(
    data => { console.log(data); },
    error => { console.error('ERROR', error); }
  );
```

Instead, you should tell the HttpClient that you expect a 'blob' (binary data) as the response type:

```typescript
import { HttpClient } from '@angular/common/http';
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';

@Injectable({
  providedIn: 'root'
})
export class AvatarService {

  constructor(private http: HttpClient) { }

  fetchAvatarGlb(avatarId: string): Observable<Blob> {
    const url = `https://api.readyplayer.me/v2/avatars/${avatarId}.glb?preview=true`;

    return this.http.get(url, { responseType: 'blob' }).pipe(
      catchError(this.handleError)
    );
  }

  private handleError(error: HttpErrorResponse) {
    console.error('Error fetching avatar:', error);
    return throwError(() => new Error('Error fetching avatar GLB.'));
  }
}
```

Note: Replace `[avatar-id]` with the actual avatar ID.

### Handling the Blob Response

After fetching the `.glb` file as a Blob, you'll manage it according to your needs. For example, if you want to allow users to download the avatar:

```typescript
this.avatarService.fetchAvatarGlb('avatar-id').subscribe(blob => {
  // Create a URL for the blob
  const blobUrl = URL.createObjectURL(blob);

  // For example, to allow the user to download the .glb file:
  const anchor = document.createElement('a');
  anchor.href = blobUrl;
  anchor.download = 'avatar.glb';
  document.body.appendChild(anchor); // Needed for Firefox
  anchor.click();

  // Clean up
  document.body.removeChild(anchor);
  URL.revokeObjectURL(blobUrl);
});
```

This code example creates a temporary URL to the `.glb` blob, initiates a download for the user, and then does some minor cleanup.

### Summary

Your error was due to treating binary data as JSON. To resolve this, ensure that your HTTP request is configured to expect a response type of 'blob' when dealing with binary content like `.glb` files. Following this approach, you should be able to correctly handle the avatar data from ReadyPlayerMe."},
