["**Task 2: Making the system compatible for Bode plot based controller design**

**a) Plot the Nyquist plot of G(s) and determine a suitable value of Kf for stability:**

```matlab
% Define the transfer function G(s)
num = [-0.0717 -1.684 -0.0853 0.0622];
den = [1 1.0604 -1.1154 -0.066 -0.0512];
G = tf(num, den);

% Plot Nyquist plot
figure;
nyquist(G);
title('Nyquist Plot of G(s)');
grid on;

% From the Nyquist plot, determine Kf such that the closed-loop system
% G_tilde = Kf*G/(1 + Kf*G) is stable.
% This typically involves ensuring the Nyquist plot of Kf*G does not encircle -1.
% You can use the margin function to estimate gain margin and select Kf accordingly.

[GM, PM, Wcg, Wcp] = margin(G);
fprintf('Gain Margin: %f dB
', 20*log10(GM));

% Convert gain margin to linear scale
Kf = GM * 0.9; % Choose Kf slightly less than gain margin for stability
fprintf('Selected Kf for stability: %f
', Kf);

% Define the effective inner loop transfer function
G_tilde = feedback(Kf*G, 1);
```

**b) Plot the step response and pole-zero map of \( \tilde{G}(s) \) to verify stability:**

```matlab
figure;
step(G_tilde);
title('Step Response of \tilde{G}(s)');
grid on;

figure;
pzmap(G_tilde);
title('Pole-Zero Map of \tilde{G}(s)');
grid on;

% Check poles for stability
poles_tilde = pole(G_tilde);
if any(real(poles_tilde) > 0)
    disp('The system \tilde{G}(s) is unstable (poles in RHP).');
else
    disp('The system \tilde{G}(s) is stable (all poles in LHP).');
end
```

**c) Check if the step response of \( \tilde{G}(s) \) meets the design criteria:**

- Observe the step response plot for settling time, overshoot, and steady-state error.
- Use MATLAB functions such as `stepinfo` to quantify these metrics.

```matlab
info = stepinfo(G_tilde);
fprintf('Settling Time: %f seconds
', info.SettlingTime);
fprintf('Overshoot: %f%%
', info.Overshoot);
fprintf('Steady-State Value: %f
', dcgain(G_tilde));

% Compare these values with the design criteria to verify compliance.
```

This completes Task 2 by plotting the Nyquist plot, selecting a suitable gain Kf for stability, plotting the step response and pole-zero map of the closed-loop system, and checking if the step response meets the design criteria.", "Here is the full MATLAB code to perform Task 2, including plotting the Nyquist plot, selecting a suitable Kf, and plotting the step response and pole-zero map of the effective inner loop transfer function \(\tilde{G}(s) = \frac{K_f G(s)}{1 + K_f G(s)}\):

