["Let's solve Task 2 step-by-step using MATLAB commands and explanations.

### Task 2: Making the system compatible for Bode plot based controller design

**a) Plot the Nyquist plot of G(s) and determine a suitable value of Kf for stability**

```matlab
% Define the transfer function G(s)
numG = [-0.0717 -1.684 -0.0853 0.0622];
denG = [1 1.0604 -1.1154 -0.066 -0.0512];
G = tf(numG, denG);

% Plot Nyquist plot
figure;
nyquist(G);
title('Nyquist Plot of G(s)');
grid on;

% From the Nyquist plot, determine Kf such that the closed-loop system
% G_tilda = Kf*G/(1 + Kf*G) is stable.
% This typically involves ensuring the Nyquist plot of Kf*G does not encircle -1.
% You can use the 'margin' function to get gain margin (GM) and phase margin (PM):
[GM, PM, Wcg, Wcp] = margin(G);

% Gain margin GM is given as a ratio; to ensure stability, choose Kf < GM
fprintf('Gain Margin (GM): %.3f
', GM);

% Choose Kf less than GM to ensure stability
Kf = 0.5 * GM;  % Example: half the gain margin
fprintf('Chosen Kf for stability: %.3f
', Kf);

% Define the effective inner loop transfer function G_tilda
G_tilda = feedback(Kf*G, 1);

% Plot step response and pole-zero map to verify stability
figure;
step(G_tilda);
title('Step Response of G_tilda(s)');
grid on;

figure;
pzmap(G_tilda);
title('Pole-Zero Map of G_tilda(s)');
grid on;

% Check poles for stability
poles_tilda = pole(G_tilda);
if all(real(poles_tilda) < 0)
    disp('G_tilda(s) is stable with chosen Kf.');
else
    disp('G_tilda(s) is unstable with chosen Kf. Adjust Kf accordingly.');
end

% **c) Check if step response meets any of the three design criteria**
